I’d like to talk about a novel that I found extremely interesting, which is The Smiling, Proud Wanderer, written by Jin Yong, one of the most famous martial arts novelists in Chinese literature.

I first read this novel a few years ago after one of my friends strongly recommended it to me. At first, I thought it was just another martial arts story with sword fights and legendary heroes, but after reading it, I realized that it was much deeper than that.

The story follows a swordsman named Linghu Chong, who is carefree, independent, and doesn’t care much about power or social status. Throughout the novel, he becomes involved in conflicts between different martial arts sects. At first, the so-called “orthodox” sects appear righteous and honorable, while the “evil” sects seem dangerous and cruel. However, as the story develops, the author gradually reveals that many of the supposedly righteous characters are actually hypocritical, manipulative, and obsessed with power.

What I found most fascinating was the way the novel explored human nature and politics rather than simply focusing on martial arts. For example, one character named Yue Buqun appears to be a respected and moral leader, but later turns out to be extremely ambitious and dishonest. Meanwhile, some characters from the so-called evil sect are actually more genuine and loyal than the “good” people.

I think the novel was so interesting because it challenged my perspective on concepts like good and evil. It made me realize that people and organizations are often far more complicated than they appear on the surface. Besides that, the story also contains meaningful themes about freedom, power, hypocrisy, and ambition, which are still very relevant in modern society.

Although it’s a martial arts novel, I think it’s really more of a reflection on politics and human behavior. Even now, I still remember many of the lessons and ideas from the story, which is why it left such a strong impression on me.
